// FIXED 2026-08-08 on the NODE-WALK side, as the direction note below
// predicted: a handler delivery is an outward-bound event, so
// `Qop::update` now parks it in `ExecCtx::frame_outbox` when
// `frame_depth > 0` and `GXLambda::update` drains it into the real
// `event.variables` once the frames unwind (nested frames bubble one
// level per unwind). Both engines now emit (0, 10) then (1, 10).
//
// Note on how this hid: 9d042cbc's message claims it made this program
// AGREE. It did — but by accident and for the wrong reason. That same
// commit grew `graphix_dyncall` to five parameters without updating
// `emit_qop_deliver`, so cranelift rejected the function and this
// region stopped fusing; both engines then node-walked and agreed on
// the swallowed handler. Fixing the arity
// (findings/qop-deliver-dyncall-arity-aug2026) re-exposed the real bug
// immediately, which is the argument for `fusecheck`: a de-fusion that
// silences a divergence reads exactly like a fix.
//
// ── original report ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A `?` that raises INSIDE a tail-loop iteration: the node-walk's
// handler delivery is swallowed by the frame-private variables map, so
// the catch handler never runs. The JIT delivers it.
//
//   interp: (0, 10)              — n stays 0 forever
//   jit:    (0, 10) then (1, 10) — the handler fires, n becomes 1
//
// The loop is `go(2, 0)`: iteration i=2 evaluates `10 /? (2-1)` = 10
// (fine) and iteration i=1 evaluates `10 /? 0`, which raises. Both
// backends agree on the RESULT (10 — the raise bottoms that iteration's
// contribution), and disagree only about whether the handler saw the
// error.
//
// Handler-independent confirmation (rules out `count`):
//   let tag = "none";
//   catch(e) tag <- e ~ "caught";
//   let rec go = |i: i64, acc: i64| -> i64 select i {
//     i64:0 => acc,
//     _ => go(i - i64:1, acc + (i64:10 /? (i - i64:1))?)
//   };
//   let r = go(i64:2, i64:0);
//   println("tag=[tag] r=[r]")
// --no-fusion prints `tag=none r=10`; fusion prints `tag=none r=10`
// then `tag=caught r=1`.
//
// Mechanism (node-walk side). A re-entered tail pass runs against a
// PRIVATE variables map — the evaluation FRAME (node/lambda.rs ~480-520,
// design/replay_frames.md): "the body run against a PRIVATE variables
// map ... under a forced init view". `Qop::update`'s handler path
// delivers the error by WRITING the catch handler's variable into
// `event.variables`. Inside a frame that write lands in the frame's
// private map, which is discarded when the pass ends, so the delivery
// never reaches the real event and the handler is never woken. The
// kernel has no frame — a tail loop is a rebind-and-jump inside one
// invocation — so its QopDeliver writes the real variable.
//
// This is the same family as frame-state-cross-cycle-jul2026 ("frame
// state cannot survive across cycles" — Eric's ruling 2026-07-16), but
// the opposite direction: here something that must ESCAPE the frame
// (an error delivery to a handler OUTSIDE the lambda) is trapped by it.
//
// Fix: a handler delivery is not frame state — it is an outward-bound
// event, like a `<-` to a binding outside the lambda. The frame's
// private map must not capture writes to ids that are not frame-local:
// when the qop's `handler_id` is not in the frame's seeded set, write it
// through to the real `event.variables` (the same distinction
// `seed_externals` already draws when it seeds the frame from the
// runtime cache). Worth checking whether ordinary `<-` to an outside
// binding from inside a tail loop has the same hole — the connect
// target is a variable write with exactly the same shape.
//
// Direction note: the NODE-WALK is the side that loses the event here.
// The kernel's behavior matches the documented semantics (the handler
// covers the `?`, so it should receive the error), so this likely wants
// a fix on the canonical side — worth Eric's confirmation.
{
  let n = i64:0;
  catch(e) n <- count(e);
  let rec go = |i: i64, acc: i64| -> i64 select i {
    i64:0 => acc,
    _ => go(i - i64:1, acc + (i64:10 /? (i - i64:1))?)
  };
  let r = go(i64:2, i64:0);
  (n, r)
}
